  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $x$ and its reciprocal $\\frac1x$ are both solutions of the quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$. If $x\\ne\\pm1$, then prove that $a=c$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=c$.\n\nSubtracting the two root equations after clearing fractions gives $(c-a)(x^2-1)=0$. Since $x\\ne\\pm1$, $c-a=0$.",
      "worked": "The two solutions satisfy\n$$ax^2+bx+c=0$$\nand\n$$\\frac{a}{x^2}+\\frac{b}{x}+c=0.$$\nMultiply the second equation by $x^2$:\n$$cx^2+bx+a=0.$$\nSubtract the first equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}(c-a)x^2+a-c&=0\\\\(c-a)x^2-(c-a)&=0\\\\(c-a)(x^2-1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nSince $x\\ne\\pm1$, $x^2-1\\ne0$. The null factor law gives $c-a=0$, so $a=c$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 92
  },
